<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghalib Alhinai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ghalib ibn Ali al-Hinai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghalib ibn Ali al-Hinai was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, elected in 1954 after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-khalili">Muhammad al-Khalili</a>. His attempts to win international recognition for the interior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, amid disputes over oil concessions, led to conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, who with British help occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> in 1955. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-akhdar-war">Jebel Akhdar War</a> ended in 1959 he went into exile in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghalib-alhinai.jpg" alt="Ghalib Alhinai" width="292" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sawt Oman Magazine, August 1962 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghalib_Alhinai_-_1962.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After al-Khalili's death, Imam <b>Ghalib ibn Ali</b> sought outside recognition as oil exploration began.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-oman">Imamate of Oman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dammam">Dammam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21,57&amp;year=1975" aria-label="Oman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6083 1387 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ghalib Alhinai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6533 1657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6533 1657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21,57&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
